A weathered woodworker's hands carefully shaping a delicate circuit board with traditional hand tools—chisels, planes, and gouges—in a warm Renaissance workshop filled with golden afternoon light streaming through arched windows, sawdust particles floating in the air, detailed sketches of software architecture pinned to wooden walls alongside anatomical drawings, the circuit board emerging organically from a block of aged oak wood, showing the grain merging into copper traces, rendered in the sfumato technique with meticulous cross-hatching and sepia tones characteristic of Leonardo da Vinci's notebook illustrations.
